WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:02.340
We're interested in

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.170
the concept of emergence.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.000
So emergence is

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.880
where functions emerge

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.180
that are not evident

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:11.660
in the individual parts.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.860
So if we think about emergence in

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.140
biological systems

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.240
the classical swarms

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:18.990
of birds flying,

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:20.910
or fish schooling.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:26.070
No one really understands how those individual
entities coordinate their actions, but this

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:30.130
has huge implications also for medicine.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:36.830
So how do cells turn into tissues, tissues
organs, and organs organisms?

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:42.000
And in fact what's really exciting about the
CIFAR program that is unusual and unique across

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:49.440
the world is we're also looking at how one
can engineer these systems so this has implications

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.740
for autonomous vehicles.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:56.579
So now you know, we're envisioning a future
state where a number of different autonomous

00:00:56.579 --> 00:01:03.430
vehicles interact with each other and we want
to avoid unexpected consequences of that interaction

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:10.460
as well as control and optimize their function
and we think there's real commonalities between

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:15.880
individual cells, individual organisms, and
now individual machines especially when those

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:22.370
machines are started to be endowed with autonomous
properties such as happens with AI and other systems.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:29.950
We think that the opportunity of act - of
understanding human-machine interfaces from

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:36.170
a cellular transplantation perspective right
through to prosthetics and finally humans

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:41.119
interacting with autonomous vehicles all depend
upon understanding two-way communication

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:47.110
between devices and humans broadly interpreted.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:51.280
And so that will enable all kinds of new applications
in those areas as well.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.150
These problems have no international boundaries.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:59.460
You know, we seek out and have brought together
the best people in the world around this problem

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:07.950
whether they're in Canada, Germany, the US,
or Japan and that really is something that

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:14.160
otherwise is tough to get the attention of
these people, and CIFAR because of its reputation,

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:19.280
because of its infrastructure is allowing
us to do that so that's a very exciting aspect.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:26.780
[Music]

